  },
  "grammar": {
    "title": "The 'would' tense: soften requests and imagine",
    "intro": "The <b>conditional</b> is Spanish's version of English <b>“would”</b>. Use it to be polite (<b>Me gustaría un café</b>), to ask gently (<b>¿Podrías ayudarme?</b>), and to imagine (<b>Sería mejor</b>). The good news: it's built almost exactly like the future, and it shares the same irregular stems.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "tag": "formación",
        "heading": "One ending for all verbs",
        "body": "Take the full <b>infinitive</b> and add the same endings to <b>-ar</b>, <b>-er</b>, and <b>-ir</b> verbs. Every ending carries an accent on the <b>í</b>:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "yo",
            "hablaría"
          ],
          [
            "tú",
            "hablarías"
          ],
          [
            "él/ella",
            "hablaría"
          ],
          [
            "nosotros",
            "hablaríamos"
          ],
          [
            "vosotros",
            "hablaríais"
          ],
          [
            "ellos",
            "hablarían"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "Yo <b>comería</b> más, pero no tengo hambre.",
            "en": "I would eat more, but I'm not hungry.",
            "note": "comer → comería"
          },
          {
            "es": "Nosotros <b>viviríamos</b> en España.",
            "en": "We would live in Spain.",
            "note": "vivir → viviríamos"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "irregulares",
        "heading": "Same irregular stems as the future",
        "body": "A handful of verbs change their stem, but the endings stay identical. If you learned the future, you already know these:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "tener",
            "tendría"
          ],
          [
            "hacer",
            "haría"
          ],
          [
            "poder",
            "podría"
          ],
          [
            "salir",
            "saldría"
          ],
          [
            "decir",
            "diría"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>Tendría</b> que preguntar a mi jefe.",
            "en": "I would have to ask my boss.",
            "note": "tener → tendr-"
          },
          {
            "es": "¿<b>Podrías</b> abrir la ventana?",
            "en": "Could you open the window?",
            "note": "poder → podr-"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "cortesía",
        "heading": "Politeness and soft requests",
        "body": "The conditional makes wishes and requests sound gentle and polite — much softer than the present tense:",
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>Me gustaría</b> un café, por favor.",
            "en": "I would like a coffee, please.",
            "note": "softer than 'quiero'"
          },
          {
            "es": "<b>Sería</b> mejor esperar.",
            "en": "It would be better to wait.",
            "note": "ser → sería"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "Conditional = infinitive + ía, ías, ía, íamos, íais, ían.",
      "Every form keeps the accent on the í: hablaría, comería.",
      "Irregular stems match the future: tendría, haría, podría, saldría, diría.",
      "Main uses: politeness (Me gustaría) and hypotheticals (Sería mejor)."
    ],
    "mistakes": [
      {
        "w": "Yo hablaria con él.",
        "r": "Yo hablaría con él.",
        "why": "Never drop the accent — the í always carries it: hablaría."
      },
      {
        "w": "Yo poderia ayudar.",
        "r": "Yo podría ayudar.",
        "why": "Poder uses the irregular stem podr-, not the full infinitive."
      },
      {
        "w": "Me gustar un café.",
        "r": "Me gustaría un café.",
        "why": "For a polite 'I would like', use the conditional gustaría."
      }
    ]
  },
